value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220265} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb27b7c-cbee-5694-b8a6-0ff89e5ad758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eb27b7c-cbee-5694-b8a6-0ff89e5ad758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220266} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f782384-4fcc-5cd6-8d12-186dee65b2e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220263} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9acf70-7997-5a6f-b4c8-3f83df800a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220268} {:answer/id #uuid "20f4acbd-2640-53d8-81a9-6ebd51d19308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220269} {:answer/id #uuid "70f6b854-eefe-5c8c-bd2a-0620953d05b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a9acf70-7997-5a6f-b4c8-3f83df800a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220268} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9370c52-5813-574d-ab8d-c0083d24441f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25037b31-106a-534b-9681-846c090e0805" :question/math-academy-id "q-332816" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(2y-x-1),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$\n  {{field-6}}." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(2y-x-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{4}{3}+1=\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}(2{y}_{p}-{x}_{\\text{new}}-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}-2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{10}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{10}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 22 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{10}{3}$ | $\\frac{11}{6}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{19}{6}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{10}{3}$ | $\\frac{11}{6}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{19}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{19}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ecbd28c-9d5a-5802-8792-6ba97651ecd2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Corrector Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c566cebd-498f-5854-b2b1-0e50be0840c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339683} {:answer/id #uuid "65987ca5-6881-52ae-868b-bcd94ec27815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65987ca5-6881-52ae-868b-bcd94ec27815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339684} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c6b895-dda4-5e2d-9679-2a9619f8ea4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f54c4f8-5ee5-5fbb-a2e3-e42e59e115dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333095" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-1$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{5}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=-1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=-1.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5aefa9-a83c-5203-8b73-c96b5f7df6bf" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using the ABM 2-Step Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc6f3fc7-f396-52f7-af33-4fe1e1f2ca4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339687} {:answer/id #uuid "c45e120f-d21f-59ca-b2e4-059a2eb3059e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339688} {:answer/id #uuid "1608dfe9-2c68-580f-93f3-c4769d942cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c45e120f-d21f-59ca-b2e4-059a2eb3059e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339688} :answer-field/id #uuid "7333a832-84eb-5e40-b199-9fa2cf81e219" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339686} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac24c307-13cd-5f1d-b78a-7b3bdd9598e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{218}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339691} {:answer/id #uuid "3acd9e19-19f3-516c-9c3b-d9ca9228ed79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{182}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339692} {:answer/id #uuid "4529f81e-fcbd-5eff-bf8d-c4e66cd625a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4529f81e-fcbd-5eff-bf8d-c4e66cd625a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339693} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f011232-5b16-515e-b172-f5566b73be67" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339690} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b316428f-25ba-577f-b941-cc6a187d5aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339695} {:answer/id #uuid "6403ffee-4d61-5ba3-b1ad-88cd4fd2403b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{63}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339696} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd2b14b-683f-5d44-b5ab-186173aab408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{46}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b316428f-25ba-577f-b941-cc6a187d5aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339695} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9a83f1-bcfb-5b8a-9ecc-e96a3dbba8c6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7da9482-fe58-5366-a4a5-d9b89c09e13c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339699} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ebda04-07fc-5075-b89f-41aef1741d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339700} {:answer/id #uuid "ff57e55f-6067-5e84-9bbb-bc6974a6e4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff57e55f-6067-5e84-9bbb-bc6974a6e4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339701} :answer-field/id #uuid "e41fdc2d-7e16-580d-a01b-2f1896584c0f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "082d8cf1-a8e2-5c1e-8c15-edabaefc9968" :question/math-academy-id "q-333262" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$ {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFrom the second row of the partially filled table, we have that $y(1)≈\\frac{8}{3},$ where the predicted $y$-increment is $Δ{y}_{p}=8.$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{8}{3}+8=\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{p} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ | $\\frac{128}{3}$ |  |\n\nAlso from the table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,\\frac{8}{3})$ is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{16}{3}.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{128}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{768}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ | $\\frac{128}{3}$ | $\\frac{64}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{64}{3}=24\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ | $\\frac{128}{3}$ | $\\frac{64}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $24$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈24.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5a37e3d-f1ce-57f2-baef-c464a4f89ad4" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using The ABM 2-Step Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0522a605-4003-527b-a8a4-eeba2d31d5b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{121}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339704} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f736d9-9da7-50ee-a79b-f372dd290f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{113}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339705} {:answer/id #uuid "d93500d6-1069-5f7b-844b-f485697c7ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{97}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339706} {:answer/id #uuid "ac18c951-6990-5883-9709-de365f28e9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{109}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339707} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa6c69a-46e2-537b-9fff-93f89b4f3efe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{105}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1f736d9-9da7-50ee-a79b-f372dd290f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{113}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339705} :answer-field/id #uuid "dae63ddd-40e2-5076-9004-0852a2184cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ce77591-cec6-565a-a809-39e62243ef30" :question/math-academy-id "q-333687" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y-10,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(2)≈-\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(3).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & ={x}_{-1}-{y}_{-1}-10 \\\\  & =1-2-10 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(2,-\\frac{9}{2})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y-10 \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{9}{2})-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{2})-(-11))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}=-\\frac{17}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p}-10 \\\\  & =3-(-\\frac{17}{4})-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-\\frac{11}{4})+8\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{2})-(-11))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-\\frac{123}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{41}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ | $-\\frac{41}{16}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}+(-\\frac{41}{16}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{113}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ | $-\\frac{41}{16}$ |\n| $3$ | $-\\frac{113}{16}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈-\\frac{113}{16}.$"}]}
